    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `RequirementPreparer._fetch_metadata_using_link_data_attr`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_fetch_metadata_using_link_data_attr` function is responsible for fetching metadata for a given package requirement using a metadata link provided by the backend. It takes an `InstallRequirement` object as a parameter, which represents the package requirement. The function first attempts to retrieve a metadata link from the requirement's link object. If no metadata link is available, it returns `None`. If a link is available, it logs a verbose message indicating the source of the metadata. It then downloads the metadata file using the `get_http_url` function, passing the metadata link, a download function, and hashes for verification. After downloading, it reads the contents of the metadata file. Using these contents, it generates a distribution object with `get_metadata_distribution`, which requires the metadata contents, the filename from the link, and the name from the requirement. The function checks if the canonicalized name from the metadata matches the canonicalized name from the requirement. If they do not match, it raises a `MetadataInconsistent` exception. If everything is consistent, it returns the metadata distribution object. This function relies on helper functions like `get_http_url` and `get_metadata_distribution` to perform specific tasks. It handles cases where no metadata link is available by returning early with `None`. It also raises an exception if there is a mismatch between the names in the metadata and the requirement. The function has no side effects other than logging and file operations.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
